course: everyday
lesson: 521
title: Foster care
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Foster carers are grown-ups who look after a child in their own home when the child's parents can't. They are checked and trained first, and a social worker visits to make sure the child is safe and well. Tayla arrives at her new foster home with everything she owns in two black bin bags. Her foster mum, Jan, takes one look and fetches a proper suitcase with wheels. «Your stuff deserves better than that, love.» Tayla doesn't know whether to trust her yet, but she keeps touching the suitcase. Trust grows slowly, and small kind acts help it grow.
explain: Every Saturday Josiah's foster mom calls up the stairs, «Y'all want pancakes?» («y'all» means you all, in the southern USA). After three months Josiah starts calling her «Mama J». He still loves his birth mom, the mom he was born to, and he sees her at contact visits. Reece sees his birth mum every two weeks at a family centre, with a worker in the room. She brings sweets and hugs him hard. He loves her and he's angry with her, both in the same hour. Loving your foster family and your birth family, and being cross too, are all allowed.
explain: Not every placement works. Cormac's foster carers shout at him and leave him at home on family days out. He's scared that if he tells, he'll be moved again. He tells his social worker anyway. She listens, writes it all down, and within a fortnight he moves to a new family, the Okafors. It's hard to start again, but he is safe, and every child has the right to be safe and treated fairly. Delphine starts at her third school in two years. «New kid again,» she thinks. «Thinking this one'll be different is delulu» («delulu» means kidding yourself). She makes one friend in the first week, and that's enough to start.
explain: Good days happen too. Mitch's foster dad runs behind his bike, holding the seat, and lets go. «You beauty, you got it!» he shouts (Australian for brilliant, well done). Kayden's foster family has a fat old Labrador called Biscuit who sleeps on Kayden's bed from the first night. «He's your dog now, eh,» says his foster dad in Toronto. Tyrese has never been on holiday. His foster carer says, «We're going to Cornwall, you're coming.» «The hotel pool's peng,» he tells his mates after (London slang for great).
explain: Ciara and her little brother Cian are in different foster homes, because no carer had room for both. They see each other every two weeks and video-call in between. She asks her social worker if they can meet more. Sometimes, the plan becomes long-term. Amara's social worker tells her, «Your foster family would like you to stay with them long-term, until you're grown up.» «Like... forever?» Amara whispers. Thrilled and scared at once, she makes a list of questions, and the social worker answers every one.
fact: Foster carers look after children in their own homes when the children's parents can't.
fact: Foster carers are checked and trained, and social workers visit to make sure children are safe.
fact: Some foster placements are short, some long-term, until the child grows up.
fact: Children in foster care often move a lot, which makes trust hard; small kind acts help trust grow.
fact: A birth parent is the parent a child was born to; contact visits let children in care see them.
fact: Children can love their foster family and their birth family, and feel angry too.
fact: Every child has the right to be safe and treated fairly; if a placement isn't safe, tell a social worker or teacher.
fact: Moving school is hard; making one friend first helps.
fact: Brothers and sisters in care are sometimes placed apart, and they should still see each other.
fact: Foster families include children in ordinary family life, like holidays, pets and birthdays.
fact: Children can ask their social worker questions, and their views are listened to.
fact: «Y'all» means you all, «delulu» means kidding yourself, «you beauty» is Australian for brilliant, «peng» is London slang for great, and «eh» is a word Canadians often add to the end of a sentence.
exercise: What do foster carers do? | Care for children at home when parents can't | Run big children's homes with lots of staff on shifts | Teach children in schools who have no parents at all | Visit children once a year to bring them presents
exercise: How did Tayla arrive? | With her things in bin bags | In a big car with a removal van | With nothing at all | With three suitcases
exercise: What did Jan do about the bin bags? | Gave Tayla a proper suitcase | Threw the bags away with the stuff | Told her to unpack in the garage | Laughed at her for having bags
exercise: Why did Tayla keep touching the suitcase without trusting Jan yet? | Trust takes time, but the kindness mattered | She wanted to pack up and leave that very night | She thought the suitcase was going to be taken away | She was checking that it really had wheels on it
exercise: Josiah calls his foster mom «Mama J». Does that mean he stopped loving his birth mom? | No, he can love both | Yes, he has to choose | Yes, that's the rule | No, but he has to hide it
exercise: What is a birth mum? | The mum a child was born to | The midwife at the hospital | The mum on a child's birthday | A mum who has twins
exercise: How did Reece feel at the contact visits? | Loving and angry at once | Only happy, the whole time | Only bored and wanting to leave | Only scared
exercise: Cormac's carers shouted and left him out. What did he do? | Told his social worker | Ran away in the night | Said nothing for years | Shouted back every day
exercise: What happened after Cormac told? | He was moved to a safer family | He was told off for telling tales | Nothing happened at all | He went back to the same carers
exercise: «Thinking this one'll be different is delulu.» What does «delulu» mean? | Kidding yourself | Being really clever | Very tired | Really lucky
exercise: How did Delphine start at her third school? | By making one friend | By making no friends at all on purpose | By telling everyone her whole life story | By asking to go back to her second school
exercise: Mitch's foster dad shouts, «You beauty!» What does he mean? | Brilliant, well done | You look pretty | Be careful | Watch out for the car
exercise: How did Kayden start to feel he belonged? | Biscuit the dog slept on his bed | His foster dad bought him a phone | He was given the biggest bedroom | He got to skip school for a week
exercise: Why are Ciara and Cian in different foster homes? | No carer had room for both | They asked to live apart | They fought too much at home | Cian is a lot older
exercise: How do Ciara and Cian stay close? | Visits every two weeks and video calls | They don't, they've forgotten each other | They write one letter a year | They only meet at Christmas
exercise: «Long-term» foster care for Amara means what? | She can stay with them until she's grown up | She stays with them for one long weekend | She'll be moved on to a new family next month | She's allowed to stay up late on Saturdays
exercise: Tyrese says the pool was «peng». What did he think? | It was great | It was cold | It was dirty | It was small
test: «foster kids are just kids nobody wants». Right? | No, their parents can't care for them right now | Yes, that's exactly why they're in care at all | Yes, unless they get adopted | No, foster kids are all orphans
test: Cormac: «if I tell they'll move me again, so I'll shut up». What is the right thing? | Tell anyway; every child has the right to be safe | Stay quiet, because being moved is always worse | Wait until he's grown up, then tell someone | Try to be so good the carers stop shouting
test: What would most likely happen if Jan had laughed at the bin bags? | Tayla would find it even harder to trust her | Tayla would trust her straight away | Tayla would laugh and feel at home | Nothing, bags don't matter to kids
test: Reece: «I love Mum but I'm so mad at her, am I weird?» What's true? | No, both feelings at once are normal | Yes, you should only feel one thing | Yes, and he must stop seeing her for a while | No, but he must hide the anger
test: In a cartoon, the foster kid is sent to live in a cupboard under the stairs. What's more real? | Carers are checked, and social workers visit | Foster kids always sleep in cupboards | No one ever checks on foster kids | Foster carers are paid to be mean
test: Ciara: «we see each other every 2 weeks but it's not enough». What can she do? | Ask her social worker if they can meet more | Run off to Cian's foster home by herself | Stop seeing him because it hurts too much | Nothing, children are never allowed to ask about that
test: ★★★ Ciara sees Cian every two weeks, starting on Saturday 1 June. On which date in June is their third meeting? | 29 June | 15 June | 22 June | 14 June
test: ★★★ Delphine started at school one in September 2024, school two in March 2025 and school three in September 2025. How long did she stay at school two? | Six months | One year | Three months | Nine months
test: Amara: «long-term?? what if I mess up and they change their minds». What's the wise move? | Write her worries down and ask the social worker | Be perfect every minute so they never change their mind | Say no so she can never be let down | Keep it to herself and hope for the best
test: Kayden's foster dad says, «He's your dog now, eh.» Where does that «eh» suggest he lives? | Canada | Spain | Japan | Kenya
test: Josiah's friend: «mama J?? she's not ur real mom, that's sus». What would Josiah most likely say? | She's real; she looks after me, and I love my birth mom too | You're right, I'll stop calling her that from today | My birth mom is fake, so I'll only love Mama J now | I'm not allowed to talk about my families at school
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises foster care? | Children are cared for in a carer's own home whilst their parents are unable to do so | Children are cared for in large institutions staffed by rotating shifts of teachers | Children are permanently removed from all contact with their parents without exception | Children are placed with any adult who volunteers, without examination or training
exam: Given that a child in foster care is treated unfairly by the carers, ascertain the appropriate course of action. | To inform the social worker or a teacher | To remain silent so as to avoid a move | To leave the placement without notice | To retaliate against the carers in kind
exam: Identify the meaning of the term «birth parent» as established in the lesson. | The parent to whom the child was born | The parent present on the child's birthday | The midwife who attended the child's birth | Any foster carer of a long-term placement
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning siblings in care. | They may be placed apart, and contact between them should be maintained | They are invariably placed together in all circumstances whatsoever | They must not be permitted to see one another once placed apart | They cease to be regarded as siblings upon placement in care
